===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[position]] on a [[scale]].
# An [[evaluation]] of [[status]], especially of financial status.
#: {{ux|en|They have a poor credit '''rating'''.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Blake Ellis and Melanie Hicken|title=How the Better Business Bureau rakes in millions|url=https://www.cnn.com/2015/09/30/news/better-business-bureau-millions/index.html|date=2015-09-30|passage=A self-proclaimed source of mediation and unbiased '''ratings''' of millions of businesses across the country, the BBB receives the majority of its revenue from membership fees paid by hundreds of thousands of companies. {{...}} But through its months-long investigation of the BBB, CNNMoney found that this '''rating''' system – a key part of what the organization’s reputation is built on – is seriously flawed, with more than 100 companies in hot water with government agencies receiving A '''ratings'''|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260102011127/https://www.cnn.com/2015/09/30/news/better-business-bureau-millions/index.html|archivedate=2 January 2026}}
# A number, letter, or other mark that refers to the ability of something.
#: {{ux|en|He has a high chess '''rating'''.}}
# A quantitative measure of the audience of a television program.
#* {{quote-book|en|date=May 9 1961|author=w:Newton N. Minow|title=w:Television and the Public Interest
|passage=A '''rating''', at best, is an indication of how many people saw what you gave them.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} A [[seaman]] in a [[warship]].
#* {{quote-web|en|year=2014|author=BBC News|title=Huge Russian warship fascinates French in Saint-Nazaire|site=bbc.com|url=http://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-28697529
|passage=Some 400 Russian '''ratings''' are living in the western French port, awaiting delivery of their controversial new command-and-control ship, the Vladivostok.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical|British}} An enlisted seaman not a commissioned officer or warrant officer.
#: {{ux|en|In the [[Royal Navy]] the '''ratings''', in order, are: [[ordinary seaman]], [[able seaman]], [[leading seaman]], [[petty officer]] and [[chief petty officer]].}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1950|author=Winston S. Churchill|chapter=The Hinge of Fate|volume=4|title=The Second World War|location=Boston|publisher=Houghton Mifflin|page=149
|passage=Fifty officers and seven hundred and fifty '''ratings''' from the two British ships were picked up by the Japanese, together with the survivors from the <I>Pope</I>}}

